{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"evileyed-depletive-flycatcher","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Problem\n\nWhen a ticket description exceeds the 8,000-character plan-review admission limit, the only\nremediation offered is **decomposition**. `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/det_floor.py:347-350`:\n\n```python\n\"suggested_fix\": (\n    f\"Reduce the description to at most {desc_limit} characters, usually by splitting \"\n    \"independent work into coherent child tickets.\"\n),\n```\n\nThat is the right advice when the **work** is too large. It is the wrong first advice when the\n**prose** is too large, which is the more common cause — and an author who follows it fragments a\ncoherent unit of work for a reason that has nothing to do with its scope.\n\n## Evidence\n\nMeasured on `darkish-enormous-raptors` (`76b2-4b5f-5682-45e5`), which hit the limit:\n\n```\ndescription at rejection : 7989 chars — 14 acceptance criteria, 105 lines of narrative prose\nafter rewriting prose out: 4440 chars — ALL 14 criteria retained, plus 9 required provenance lines\nheadroom under the limit : 3560 chars (44% spare)\n```\n\n**Nothing needed splitting.** The unit of work was one coherent cleanup with fourteen criteria. What\nexceeded the limit was method discussion, rationale, and conversational progression that belonged in\nticket comments. Removing none of the commitments and none of the evidence — only relocating the\nnarration — left 44% headroom.\n\nHad the author followed the suggested fix literally, they would have split a single cleanup into\nchild tickets to solve a formatting problem.\n\nAt least **two independent sessions hit this limit on 2026-08-20**, and both independently arrived\nat the same unsuggested remedy: move the narrative into a comment. Two sessions rediscovering the\nsame fix that the message does not mention is the signal.\n\n## Why this is a coaching defect and NOT a limit defect\n\nStated plainly, because this ticket replaces an earlier one of mine\n(`piecemeal-batiste-greatargus`, closed `not_a_bug`) that got this wrong:\n\n**8,000 characters is ample for one ticket's worth of work** — roughly 1,200 words. A plan that\ncannot state its problem and its acceptance criteria in that space is either describing more than\none unit of work or is padded. The limit is correct and should not move. The formatting requirements\nthat share the budget (`provenance:` continuation lines) are also correct and fit comfortably.\n\nThe gap is that the message names one cause of over-length and one remedy, when there are two\ndistinct causes with different remedies, and the more common one is unaddressed.\n\n## Direction (not a prescription)\n\nDistinguish the two cases and coach accordingly. The signal is already available: this same function\ncomputes `oversize signals` separately from `description_over_limit`, and its OTHER branch already\nsuggests G5 decomposition for genuinely large units. So decomposition advice already has a home;\nthe over-limit branch could instead coach on authoring:\n\n- prefer concise language over narration;\n- move conversational progression, method discussion, and rationale into ticket **comments** or a\n  **session log** — the description carries the plan and its acceptance criteria;\n- follow the packaged authoring guidance (`rebar explain plan`,\n  `src/rebar/_guides/writing-a-passing-plan.md`);\n- and only split when the WORK is genuinely more than one unit — pointing at the oversize-signal\n  branch rather than conflating the two.\n\nWhether to keep any mention of splitting in this branch is a judgement call for the implementer;\nthe defensible minimum is that it stops being the *only* suggestion.\n\n## Relationship to other tickets\n\nSame class as `bediasite-bilineate-stoat` (`fb8b-189d-97ab-4546`, closed): a gate message whose\nstated remedy under-serves the situation the author is actually in. That one was fixed by making the\nmessage name the consequence the author would otherwise discover the hard way. This is the same fix\nshape applied to a different message.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The over-limit finding's `suggested_fix` names concision and relocating narration to comments\n      or a session log as remedies, not only decomposition — verified by a test asserting the\n      message content.\n- [ ] The message points at the packaged authoring guidance rather than restating it, so the advice\n      cannot drift from the guide.\n- [ ] Decomposition advice remains available for the case it genuinely fits (a unit of work too\n      large for one session) and is distinguishable from the over-length-prose case — verified by a\n      test covering both branches.\n- [ ] The 8,000-character limit itself is UNCHANGED. This ticket does not raise it, and a change\n      that raises it does not satisfy these criteria.\n- [ ] `make lint`, `make typecheck`, and `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check`\n      (`new=0 increased=0 stale=0`) pass.","id":"1e48-ff69-13f7-4a83","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Over-limit plan message suggests only decomposition, coaching authors to split coherent work when the real fix is concision"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787288189237819001,"uuid":"2eb3ab2d-509d-426f-8a7d-ea0c283c1d00"}